      "Abstract": {
        "CopyrightInformation": "© 2015 Wiley Periodicals, Inc.",
        "AbstractText": [
          {
            "NlmCategory": "BACKGROUND",
            "Label": "INTRODUCTION",
            "content": "This meta-analysis aims to assess an association between wrist ratio (wrist thickness/wrist width) and carpal tunnel syndrome (CTS)."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
            "Label": "METHODS",
            "content": "Sixteen studies qualified for a random-effects meta-analysis."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "RESULTS",
            "Label": "RESULTS",
            "content": "Mean wrist ratio was higher in individuals with CTS compared with those without CTS [pooled mean difference 0.036, 95% confidence interval (CI) 0.025-0.046]. Pooled odds ratio (OR) of CTS for mean wrist ratio was 4.56 (95% CI 2.97-6.99), and for wrist ratio ≥0.70 vs. <0.70 it was 2.73 (95% CI 1.49-5.01). In addition, the pooled OR for a 1-unit (0.01) increase in wrist ratio was 1.12 (CI 1.09-1.16). The association between wrist ratio and CTS did not differ between men and women. Moreover, there was no evidence of publication bias."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
            "Label": "CONCLUSIONS",
            "content": "This meta-analysis suggests that a square-shaped wrist is a predictor for CTS in both men and women. Future studies should explore whether a square-shaped wrist can potentiate the adverse effects of obesity and occupational workloads on CTS."
          }
        ]
      },
